@*
    A game's own name, isolated from the layout around it.

    Two attributes and one element, and all three are doing work (i18n B2):

    `<bdi>` is the element designed for user-supplied text of unknown direction inside a
    fixed-direction layout, and it is why an Arabic game name cannot drag the count in the next cell
    to the wrong side of the row. Without it the name, the neutral characters beside it and the
    number renegotiate order as one bidirectional run, and the row comes apart. With it the name
    reads right-to-left *inside its own box* while the row stays left-to-right — which is the whole
    of "RTL text, LTR layout" and needs no mirrored stylesheet.

    `lang` gives a screen reader the right voice, so 北大侠客行 is pronounced as Chinese instead of
    attempted with English phonetics, and it is what selects between the regional Han forms a font
    draws. It is set only where the script settles it; see NameScript for the cases it refuses.

    `translate="no"` is not here on purpose. A game's name is its name and a browser's page
    translator should leave it alone — but that is the machine voice's rule, and the name is the one
    game-supplied string a reader might legitimately want transliterated. The strings that carry it
    are the ones where translation destroys evidence: hostnames, codebase strings, versions,
    protocol acronyms and connect-screen output.
*@

@if (Ascii)
{
    @*
        Pure ASCII, which is most of the catalogue: no element and no attributes. The page's own
        lang already describes it, and five hundred rows repeating that is the duplication this
        whole pass exists to remove.
    *@
    @Name
}
else
{
    <bdi lang="@Language" dir="@Direction">@Name</bdi>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    private bool Ascii => NameScript.IsPlainAscii(Name);

    /// <summary>Null leaves the attribute off, which is what an unsettled script gets.</summary>
    private string? Language => NameScript.LanguageOf(Name);

    private string? Direction => NameScript.DirectionOf(Name);
}
